    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="22" relname="preparation">Intuitiveness in Design</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="33" relname="background">According the The AmP6an Heritage® Dictionary of the English Language, Intuitiveness is possessing or demonstrating intuition.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="33" relname="span">In today's tech savvy world, the concept of intuitive design is no longer just a concept of perceptivity.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="5" relname="antithesis">While designing new interfaces, the designers not only think of an inbuilt sixth sense for the application</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="35" relname="span">but also try to provide additional functionality to make a product smarter.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="38" relname="span">I believe that this concept muddles this concept</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="36" relname="span">cause it limits the target audience to non computer/net savvy customers.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="37" relname="span">The customers of most application differ by the extent of PRE-knowledge they have or don't have.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="8" relname="elaboration">What may appear as intuitive to a designer of an application software or webpage may seem rather complicated to the novice user.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="24" relname="antithesis">Keeping a product just intuitive is not possible.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="25" relname="span">It is essential to incorporate a sense of innate knowledge with smart knowledge.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="31" relname="span">Sometimes it may be easier to do something in an interface in 5 steps than to use the so called built in intuitive functionality,</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="12" relname="elaboration">it all depends on who is using the application.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="26" relname="nonvolitional-cause">As the customers range from novice to experts,</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="26" relname="span">so does the ability of an application to serve a balance.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="28" relname="concession">Keeping it easy is always essential</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="28" relname="span">but to cater to a broader more knowledgeble crowd, building quick shortcuts may be essential.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="29" relname="span">A simple to use application such as MS Excel can be extremely confusing for those users who are not familiar with macro's and basic formulae.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="20" relname="concession">There may be many ideas of intuitiveness built in,</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="30" relname="span">but the question of how to achieve a simple task may still be struggle for many.</segment>
        <group id="21" type="span"/>
